      "n": 42,
      "body": "Modernism, an artistic movement in vogue from the late 19th to the early 20th century, and modernity as resistance to the medieval order after the Enlightenment are distinguished from each other, yet both commonly use the term 'modern.' It is said that the term 'modern,' unlike today, was used to evaluate negatively the forces that criticized the existing order. Ultimately, the use of 'modern' as 'the maximization of subjectivity' may be connected with the concept of the French word mondain, which shares an etymology with the Dionysian, Eastern world of nothingness (mu). The meaning of 'the secular' possessed by the French mondain may be said to be the practical meaning of 'now, here' possessed by the Latin mode. In the end, 'mondain,' which had been a term opposing the old-world order, has today conversely been mistaken for the meaning of 'the modern,' the existing order. The place where the term modernism was first used is also said to have been French avant-garde artists. Park Yong-suk holds that the origin of 'mondain' being used in the negative sense of 'the secular' stems from ancient Plato being criticized by figures such as Aristotle. It is said that from antiquity until Plato, Europe was the civilization of Venus, the origin of 'modern' and the astronomical origin of Yin-Yang and the Five Phases. (박용숙, 『샤먼문명 (별과 우주를 사랑한 지동설의 시대)』, 소동, 2015, 16-22쪽) Indeed, it is said that at the height of Rome's power, the power of the ruling class was swayed by astrology.(벤슨 보브릭, 『점성술로 되짚어보는 세계사』, 이상근 역, 서울 : 까치글방, 2006, 45-90쪽)"
